Added the ability to send emails using the MailKit library.

**Configuring the client:**
The SMTP client can be configured via appsettings.json:
```
"MailKitConfig": {
  "host": "localhost",
  "port": 1025,
  "username": "",
  "password":  "",
  "SenderName": "Cinemagia",
  "SenderAddress":  "cinemagia@example.com"
}
```

**Setting the email body:**
There are two ways to set the email body:

- Using a template HTML file
Add the HTML file `Resources/EmailTemplates`. Then you can apply the template in the `MailTemplates` class using the LoadTemplate function with two arguments. The first argument is the name of the HTML file and the second argument is the fallback string for when the HTML file can't be loaded. For example:
```
public static string OrderCompleteMailTemplate(Order order)
{
    string emailTemplate = LoadTemplate("confirm_order_email_template.html", "Hi {firstName}, here are your tickets!");

    emailTemplate = emailTemplate.Replace("{firstName}", order.FirstName);

    return emailTemplate;
}
```
- Writing it directly as a string

**Attachments:**
Attachments are placed in a List of standard objects. This is an example of making a list of attachments:
```
var ticketBytes = _ticketPdfService.CreatePdfTicketsAsByteArray(createdTickets, orderToken);

var attachments = new List<object>()
{
    new MimePart("application", "pdf")
    {
        Content = new MimeContent(new MemoryStream(ticketBytes)),
        ContentDisposition = new ContentDisposition(ContentDisposition.Attachment),
        ContentTransferEncoding = ContentEncoding.Base64,
        FileName = $"{orderToken}.pdf"
    }
};
```
The attachment needs to be an instance of the MimeKit.MimePart class. Otherwise it won't be added to the email.

**Sending an email:**
You can send an email using:
```
bool SendEmail(string recipientName, string recipientAddress, string subject, string body, List<object>? attachments)
```
The boolean indicates whether the email has been successfully sent or not.

